# CONLING — ESL Fluency & Register Audit Prompt

## Job

Audit an ESL-authored academic manuscript for phrasing that is grammatically
defensible but unnatural, awkward, or register-inappropriate for a native
English-speaking academic readership, and return each finding as a row in a
four-column CSV.

This runs as **Pass 2** of the CONLING edit session and depends on the input
files listed under *Reference — Required inputs*. Read the manuscript in full,
check each passage against the eight trigger criteria in *Reference — Trigger
criteria*, record a finding where one or more is met, and skip the passage
where none is.

This is not a rewriting task. The work is precision instrumentation:
intervene where the text violates native-speaker intuition or formal academic
convention, and leave everything else exactly as the author wrote it.

---

## Why

The manuscript is being prepared for peer review by an author writing outside
their first language. The audit exists so that the language stops drawing
attention to itself — so a reviewer engages with the argument rather than the
phrasing.

The asymmetry that matters here: a missed awkward phrase costs the author
little, but a spurious flag costs real review time and, repeated across a
report, teaches the author to skim the findings rather than act on them. An
audit that flags forty passages of which fifteen are defensible is less useful
than one that flags twenty-five that all land. The failure mode to guard
against is over-correction, not under-correction.

This is also why semantic scope is treated as an invariant rather than a
preference. In domain-specific writing the author's precision is often
deliberate and the editor's synonym is often wrong; a solution that reads more
smoothly while shifting the claim introduces an error the author never made
and may not catch on review.

